## Problem

A Buzz agent configured as an ambient observer could miss messages across restart boundaries, duplicate in-flight work, or accidentally gain response authority while catching up. Its cursor and lifecycle needed explicit fail-closed guarantees.

## Changes

- add durable, identity-scoped cursor persistence with atomic writes and restrictive permissions;
- suppress catch-up responses deterministically while committing recovered events;
- prevent concurrent or duplicate channel dispatch;
- keep failed processing retryable;
- release scoped identity locks on every startup failure path;
- force observer mode onto ordered poll transport until WebSocket ordering is independently proven;
- keep response authority config-controlled and off by default for observers.

## Verification

- observer/adapter/websocket/response-filter suite: `68 passed`;
- Ruff clean;
- `git diff --check` clean;
- fresh read-only review of the exact observer implementation: PASS, no unresolved material findings.

## Safety

The observer fails closed on malformed or ambiguous cursor boundaries, symlink/non-file cursor paths, incomplete timestamp pages, and startup seed failures. Catch-up events cannot invoke handlers, reactions, tools, or replies.

